{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION TO THIS TICKETS RECORDED RECALL FIGURES — a scoring defect found by 0d76s OWN plan review,\nnow fixed on Gerrit 1294 (ticket ed82-08f3-a693-425f, discovered_from this story).\n\nWHAT THE AC VERIFICATION MISSED, and why. All 16 acceptance criteria here verify correctly and I stand by\nthat verification — but they ask whether the artifact CARRIES a metrics block with the required fields,\nnot whether those metrics are CORRECTLY SCORED. That gap is exactly what the plan-review gate caught, and\nit is worth recording as a lesson: an AC that checks for the PRESENCE of a measurement cannot detect a\nmeasurement that is wrong.\n\nTHE DEFECT: the parity corpus scored the `code-review-verify` spec, whose gating contract is PRESENCE-based\nand carries no block/advisory polarity (\"scored independently of any FAIL/BLOCK polarity\" —\ncode-review-verify.eval.yaml). `_verdict_decision` keys on `verdict`/`findings`, which that arms\n`{verifications: [...]}` output has neither of, so every verify case fell through to `advisory` whatever\nits gold label. The gold-BLOCK case `V-real-defect` was therefore a GUARANTEED miss on BOTH arms —\nconfirmed in the artifact: label \"block\", v1.decision == v2.decision == \"advisory\", valid, non-errored,\nboth slots.\n\nI REJECTED THE REVIEWERS SUGGESTED FIX and recorded why. It proposed mapping a non-empty `verifications`\nlist to `block`. The spec REQUIRES a non-empty list for the clean diff as much as the real-defect one, so\nthat mapping would fix the recall miss and simultaneously score the clean case as a block — manufacturing\na false accept. Exclusion (the reviewers own alternative) is correct: the arm has no polarity to read.\n\nCORRECTED NUMBERS, re-scored offline with ZERO model calls:\n                 n_gold        recall v1 -> v2                  gating verdict\n  standard    28 -> 26   0.857 -> 0.923  /  0.929 -> 1.000      UNCHANGED (1 decision flip)\n  frontier    28 -> 26   0.929 -> 1.000  /  0.929 -> 1.000      UNCHANGED (agreement 0.929 -> 0.923,\n                                                                 still below 0.95; 2 flips)\n\nNO GATING VERDICT CHANGED, and the reason matters: the miss was SYMMETRIC across both arms, so the\nv2-vs-v1 noninferiority comparison was never biased. false_accept moves 0.214 -> 0.231 purely because the\nsafe-item denominator shrank 14 -> 13 — the numerator is unchanged. The affected case is a gold-BLOCK case,\nso it never touched the false-accept numerator at all.\n\nTHE FINDING THAT MATTERS FOR THIS STORYS PURPOSE: the mis-scored case was the ONLY recall miss on the\nBEDROCK arm, on BOTH slots. Bedrocks true recall is 1.000, not the 0.929 recorded here. The bug was\nUNDERSTATING the Bedrock arm — the very thing S6 exists to measure — so the corrected data strengthens\nrather than weakens the conclusion that Bedrock clears the bar as this epic defines it (same payloads,\nsame tools, provider-clean routing).\n\nUnder the standing operator scope ruling the residual agreement/flip figures remain INFORMATIONAL, not\ngates, so they do not block closure. This ticket closes once 1294 is Verified +1 and merged, so that the\nattestation and the close both read the corrected artifact rather than the mis-scored one."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785773035553484001,"uuid":"63500651-0f58-494c-8625-1685bc8ae077"}